g moderate politicians in the dust and worrying many Germans. Political psychology helps us understand what's behind growing polarization.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Could flattering AI make humanity turn on itself?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8347d346899a7978b591529abce9ccdeb52c8f1b59047fe1a0a1040061f549c9</id><updated>2026-10-07T06:11:44Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T13:10:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/could-flattering-ai-make-humanity-turn-on-itself/a-79377894--285f77843c72.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/could-flattering-ai-make-humanity-turn-on-itself/a-79377894?maca=en-rss-en-all-1573-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">A study shows chatbots mirror users' politics, part of a broader tendency known as AI sycophancy. Researchers fear this could deepen polarization.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Berlin officially withdraws Olympics bid</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d008a17c2cef94230b5c3d50bef1fdc69028ba3b6d131eb4cdc7f2e3d3a7233d</id><updated>2026-10-07T06:11:44Z</updated><published>2026-09-24T12:42:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-berlin-officially-withdraws-olympics-bid/live-79406294--30622a12ce08.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-berlin-officially-withdraws-olympics-bid/live-79406294?maca=en-rss-en-all-1573-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Berlin's decision to withdraw its Olympic bid means that only Munich and the western Rhine/Ruhr region are left in the running as potential Olympic hosts. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Could a Left Party government in Berlin expropriate large real estate companies?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7e4363b570f06c04bb0f6c6d48c2cee8d5e52f9b510a6ca22bf99349793c3885</id><updated>2026-10-07T06:11:44Z</updated><published>2026-09-23T14:49:00Z</published><l